<speak>
chapter 14. i’m your sister, not your mom.
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
healing your relationship with your parents is only half the story. because while you were carrying their wounds, you were also carrying your siblings. you became their third parent, their shield, their fixer. and while they got a childhood, you often got a job.
<break time="600ms"/>
this chapter is about resetting that dynamic. about grieving what you missed, naming the resentment, and finally stepping back into the role you were meant to have: just being their sister.
<break time="600ms"/>
the childhood split screen. draw two columns:
<break time="600ms"/>
their childhood: cartoons, birthday parties, protection, freedom.
<break time="600ms"/>
my childhood: mediating fights, checking mom’s mood, cooking, responsibility.
<break time="600ms"/>
for each column, write how you feel about it.
<break time="600ms"/>
what do you still resent your sibling for - and can you name it without blaming them?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> pause here. there's no rush. press play when you're ready to continue. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
what did you shield your siblings from that nobody ever saw?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> stop the audio for a moment and write your answer. come back whenever you're ready. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
write a letter to your siblings you don’t need to send it. the power is in naming it.
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> pause the audio here, take your time with this, and press play when you're ready. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
for one month, interact with your siblings only as a sister:
<break time="600ms"/>
no fixing. no mediating. no rescuing. share one fun memory when they call.
<break time="600ms"/>
what shifted in the relationship dynamic?
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" /> take a moment. pause here, write what comes up, and come back when you're ready. <break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
the sibling challenge (family gathering edition) at the next family event, practice:
<break time="600ms"/>
don’t manage emotions.
<break time="600ms"/>
allow awkward silences.
<break time="600ms"/>
leave when you’re tired.
<break time="600ms"/>
how did it feel to stop being “the glue”?
<break time="600ms"/>
remember this...
<break time="600ms"/>
you are not your siblings’ parent. you are their sister. stepping out of the “third parent” role doesn’t abandon them, it gives both of you the chance to build a real relationship.
<break time="600ms"/>
and once you’ve put down the weight of parenting your parents and your siblings, you might find yourself asking:
<break time="600ms"/>
“now what? who am i without all the responsibility?”
<break time="600ms"/>
that’s where we end: not with duty, not with fixing, but with joy. with laughter. with play. with the little girl inside you who never stopped waiting for her turn.
</speak>
